  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Argenta, originally Montana City, sprang up in 1865 as one of the first mining camps in Montana’s Blue Wing District.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/127437",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1866 the Louis & Montana Mining Company built Montana’s first smelter in Argenta, turning the town from a placer camp into an industrial center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/127437",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s name, Argenta, comes from the French word “argentin,” meaning silvery, chosen to honor the silver‑lead deposits that outshone placer gold.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/127437",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A small historic cemetery, documented by the Montana Historical Society, contains about forty burials and stands as one of the most complete records of Argenta’s human history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://treasurestate.com/ghost-towns/argenta/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Early prospectors reached the Rattlesnake Creek area in the early 1860s, drawn by the gold fever sweeping southwestern Montana.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://treasurestate.com/ghost-towns/argenta/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Today, Argenta remains a quiet valley in the Beaverhead‑Deerlodge National Forest, with mine shafts, slag heaps, and a handful of residents keeping the ghost town alive.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        13,
        12
      ],
